From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: nds-ports <nds-ports@localhost>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 15:34:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Render with the DS 3D hardware

Replaces the software renderer with one on the DS geometry engine
(r_nds.c) and moves the 2D drawing to the touch screen, which makes the
game fit in the 4 MiB of the DS and run 10 times as fast.

- The top screen shows the 3D view: world, brush and alias models,
  sprites and particles. Vertices are 16-bit fixed point world
  coordinates, surfaces are drawn as quad strips across their polygons.
- Textures stay 8-bit in VRAM banks A, B and D, uploaded at the largest
  mip level that fits when a map loads, with the texture coordinates
  scaled to their size by the texture matrix. The palette goes to the
  texture palette, so palette shifts stay free. It's twice as bright,
  so the vertex light can overbright like in the software renderer.
- Light maps become vertex light per light style, so styles animate.
  Model lighting uses the vertex light of the nearest vertex.
- The world is traversed in fixed point with frustum clip flags, alias
  models pack their texture coordinates once per map and light each
  vertex once, and their light is cached until they move.
- Quake's 2D drawing (status bar, console, menus, messages) stays in
  software, in a 320x200 buffer scaled to the touch screen. Only the
  rows the draw functions changed are copied to VRAM.

To fit in memory the vertexes, edges, surfedges and light maps of brush
models are freed once the hardware vertices are built, alias skins are
read from the model file when a map loads instead of kept, the surface
data is one allocation, planes aren't allocated twice, the pack
directory isn't on the stack, the thread stack is 64 KiB, particles are
512, libc gets 64 KiB and small heaps get 448 edicts.

For speed memcpy and memset copy words from ITCM instead of newlib's
bytes in Thumb mode, the collision code runs from ITCM, AngleVectors
uses float trigonometry and the sound driver only submits new samples.
vid_showfps shows the frame rate on the touch screen.
